==Old Saxon==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|osx}} {{recons|wurmiz|lang=gem-pro}}, from a suffixed form of {{etyl|gem-pro|osx}} {{recons|wer-||turn, bend|lang=gem-pro}}. Cognate with Old Frisian {{term|wirm|lang=ofs}}, Old English {{term|wyrm|lang=ang}}, Old High German {{term|wurm|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Wurm|lang=de}}), Old Norse {{term|ormr|lang=non}} (Swedish {{term|orm||serpent|lang=sv}}), Gothic {{term|𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌼𐍃||worm, serpent|tr=waurms|lang=got|sc=Goth}}, Dutch {{term|worm|lang=nl}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin {{term|vermis||worm|lang=la}}, Lithuanian {{term|varmas||midge|lang=lt}}, Old East Slavic ''вермие (vermije)'' ‘locusts, worms’, Ancient Greek {{term|ῥόμος||earthworm|tr=rhomos|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}} (originally *{{term||ϝράμος|tr=wramos|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/wurm/|lang=osx}}

===Noun===
{{osx-noun|g=m}}

# a [[serpent]] or [[snake]]
# a creeping insect, maggot, grub, or [[worm]]

